  "query_preview": "..\n\nAn unsettled/active weather pattern is expected to set up over \nsoutheast Michigan through this week as we reside on the north end \nof a flattened mid/upper level ridge. The center of this ridge will \nbe slow moving across the mid Mississippi River Valley and over the \nOH/TN River valleys over the next several days. Quasi-zonal mid-\nlevel flow and southwesterly low level flow will allow for a \npersistent warm and moist airmass across the region. This will help \nsupport near daily shower and thunderstorm chances throughout most of\nthe week as shortwaves waves and frontal/baroclinic zones capitalize\non the favorable warm and humid environment. \n\nFor today, a warm front will be the focus for scattered showers this \nmorning into the afternoon. There will be a decreasing precipitation \ntrend with time today as the front is drawn northward by the \nprogression of the Midwest low pressure system. Low level jet \nforcing over the front will kick off the highest coverage of chance \nPoPs this morning through 18Z this afternoon. Not expecting a severe \nthunderstorm threat as instability will be limited with poor CAPE \ndepth and MUCAPE values of less than 500 j/kg. The threat of heavy \nrainfall has decreased to some degree with this additional round of \nprecipitation across the Thumb, especially with the break in \nprecipitation last evening. QPF through 18Z this afternoon offers \n0.25-0.50\" of rainfall with some model support for up to an inch \npossible across the portions of the Thumb. Warmer airmass pushing \ninto southeast Michigan today with 850 mb temperatures towards 20C \nwill support highs towards the mid 80s if enough clearing can occur \npost morning activity. \n\nBetter instability will build across the area on Tuesday with a weak\ncap present. HREF highlights mean surface CAPE values increasing to \n2000+ j/kg during the afternoon. Steeper mid-level lapse to around 7C\nand 0-6km bulk shear values of 30-45 knots will be more than \nsupportive of more organized convection if any activity is able to \ndevelop. Hi- res models showing a bit of variety in both timing and \noverall convective response, so Tuesday still carries a degree of \nuncertainty with the better forcing mechanism ill-timed with the \nbetter diurnal instability. Given the favorable environment, any \nstorm that can develop should be able to mature and be capable of a \nsevere wind and hail threat along with heavy downpours. SPC has \nmaintained a Marginal Risk for severe weather across all of southeast\nMichigan given the favorable environment. \n\nWednesday looks like the best chance to be the warmest day of the \nweek if the higher coverage of clouds and precipitation hold further \nto the north. This could push temperatures in the afternoon to around\n90 degrees. As with most of the days this week, any higher coverage \nof precipitation and clouds will temper NBM forecast highs. Cold \nfront and height falls forecast to arrive late Wednesday \nafternoon into Thursday bringing additional chances for showers \nand thunderstorms. Favorable environment for strong to severe \nthunderstorms will again be in place with MLCAPE values to 2000+ \nj/kg, steep mid-level lapse rates, and favorable shear that will \npose a wind and hail risk.",
